<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Br. J. Cancer</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10027333</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2362413</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6690091</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.bjc.6690091</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Low tumour cell proliferation at the invasive margin is associated with a poor prognosis in Dukes' stage B colorectal cancers</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Palmqvist</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sellberg</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>&#x000d6;berg</surname><given-names>&#x000c5;</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Tavelin</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ruteg&#x000e5;rd</surname><given-names>J N</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff4">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Stenling</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1">Department of Pathology, Ume&#x000e5; University, Ume&#x000e5;, Sweden</aff><aff id="aff2">Department of Surgery, Ume&#x000e5; University, Ume&#x000e5;, Sweden</aff><aff id="aff3">Department of Oncology, Ume&#x000e5; University, Ume&#x000e5;, Sweden</aff><aff id="aff4">Department of Surgery, District Hospital of &#x000d6;rnskoldsvik, &#x000d6;rnsk&#x000f6;ldsvik, Sweden</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="caf1"><label>*</label>, Ume&#x000e5; University, Department of Pathology, SE-901 87 Ume&#x000e5;, Sweden</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>2</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>79</volume><issue>3-4</issue><fpage>577</fpage><lpage>581</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>19</day><month>01</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>06</day><month>05</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>16</day><month>06</month><year>1998</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1999</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>The conflicting results about the prognostic impact of tumour cell proliferation in colorectal cancer might be explained by the heterogeneity observed within these tumours. We have investigated whether a systematic spatial heterogeneity exists between different compartments, and whether the presence of such a systematic heterogeneity has any impact on survival. Fifty-six Dukes' stage B colorectal cancers were carefully morphometrically quantified with respect to the immunohistochemical expression of the proliferative marker Ki-67 at both the luminal border and the invasive margin. The proliferative activity was significantly higher at the luminal border compared with the invasive margin (<italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001), although the two compartments were also significantly correlated with each other. Tumours with low proliferation at the invasive margin had a significantly poorer prognosis both in univariate (<italic>P</italic> = 0.014) and in multivariate survival analyses (<italic>P</italic> = 0.042). We conclude that Dukes' B colorectal cancers exhibit a systematic spatial heterogeneity with respect to proliferation, and tumours with low proliferation at the invasive margin had a poor prognosis. The present data independently confirm recent results from the authors, and provide new insights into the understanding of tumour cell proliferation in colorectal cancer. &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>cell proliferation</kwd><kwd>Ki-67</kwd><kwd>heterogeneity</kwd><kwd>invasion</kwd><kwd>colorectal carcinoma</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>